# 9. Arithmetic operators

You have rules such as:

```c
"+"  { printf("Arithmetic operator: +\n"); }
"-"  { printf("Arithmetic operator: -\n"); }
"*"  { printf("Arithmetic operator: *\n"); }
"/"  { printf("Arithmetic operator: /\n"); }
"%"  { printf("Arithmetic operator: %%\n"); }
```

These recognize arithmetic operators.

| Input | Meaning        |
| ----- | -------------- |
| `+`   | Addition       |
| `-`   | Subtraction    |
| `*`   | Multiplication |
| `/`   | Division       |
| `%`   | Modulus        |

For example:

```c
a + b
```

produces something like:

```text
Identifier: a
Arithmetic operator: +
Identifier: b
```

### Why `%%` in printf?

This is important.

Inside a C `printf()` format string:

```c
"%"
```

has a special meaning.

To print an actual `%`, you write:

```c
"%%"
```

Therefore:

```c
printf("Arithmetic operator: %%\n");
```

prints:

```text
Arithmetic operator: %
```

