# 21. Integer constants

This is a **regular expression**:

```c
[0-9]+
```

It means:

> One or more digits from 0 to 9.

Examples:

```text
1
25
100
999
```

are matched.

Then:

```c
printf("Integer constant: %s\n", yytext);
```

prints the actual number.

---

# 22. What is `yytext`?

This is one of the most important things to understand in Lex.

`yytext` is a Lex/Flex predefined variable that contains the **actual text matched by the current pattern**.

Suppose the input is:

```c
123
```

and this pattern matches:

```c
[0-9]+
```

Then:

```text
yytext = "123"
```

So:

```c
printf("Integer constant: %s\n", yytext);
```

prints:

```text
Integer constant: 123
```

Similarly, if the input is:

```c
hello
```

and the identifier rule matches it:

```c
[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
```

then:

```text
yytext = "hello"
```

---

# 23. Floating-point constants

You have:

```c
[0-9]+\.[0-9]+
```

This matches values such as:

```text
3.14
10.5
100.25
```

The `\.` means a **literal dot**.

So:

```text
[0-9]+
```

means one or more digits.

```text
\.
```

means a decimal point.

```text
[0-9]+
```

means one or more digits after the decimal point.

Together:

```text
digits + decimal point + digits
```

---

# 24. Character constants

You have:

```c
\'[a-zA-Z0-9]\'
```

This recognizes character constants such as:

```c
'a'
'B'
'5'
```

The structure is:

```text
' + one alphanumeric character + '
```

---

# 25. String constants

This is one of the more complicated regular expressions:

```c
\"([^\"\\]|\\.)*\"
```

It recognizes strings such as:

```c
"Hello"
"Hello World"
"Value = 10"
```

The purpose is to allow characters inside quotes and also escaped characters.

For example:

```c
"Hello\n"
```

can be recognized as a string.

You don't need to memorize this entire expression initially. The important idea is:

> It recognizes a sequence enclosed within double quotes.

---

# 26. Identifiers

This is another very important regular expression:

```c
[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
```

It recognizes C-style identifiers.

Examples:

```text
x
number
count
total_sum
_marks
student123
```

The first character must be:

```text
a-z
A-Z
_
```

After that, you can have:

```text
a-z
A-Z
0-9
_
```

That's why:

```text
student123
```

is valid.

But:

```text
123student
```

is not recognized as an identifier.

---

# 27. Ignoring spaces and newlines

You have:

```c
[ \t\n]+ ;
```

This means:

> Ignore spaces, tabs, and newlines.

For example:

```c
switch ( x )
```

The spaces don't represent useful tokens for your analyzer, so Lex simply ignores them.

The semicolon after the pattern is effectively an **empty action**.

---

# 28. Unknown symbols

Finally:

```c
. {
    printf("Unknown symbol: %s\n", yytext);
}
```

The `.` means:

> Match any single character that hasn't been matched by an earlier rule.

For example, if the input contains:

```text
@
```

and you don't have an `@` rule, you'll get:

```text
Unknown symbol: @
```

This acts as a **catch-all rule**.

---

# 29. The most important concept: rule priority

This is very important for understanding how the program works.

Flex generally follows:

> **Longest match wins. If two rules match the same amount of text, the earlier rule wins.**

For example:

```c
>=
```

There are rules for:

```text
>
>=
```

When Flex sees:

```text
>=
```

it chooses:

```text
>=
```

because it is the **longer match**.

Similarly:

```c
++
```

is matched as:

```text
++
```

rather than:

```text
+
+
```

because `++` is the longer match.

---

# 30. What happens with keywords and identifiers?

This is particularly important.

You have:

```c
"switch"
```

and later:

```c
[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
```

Both can potentially match:

```text
switch
```

Both have length 6.

So Flex uses the rule appearing **earlier**.

Therefore:

```text
switch
```

is recognized as:

```text
[SWITCH] switch statement found
```

rather than:

```text
Identifier: switch
```

The same applies to:

```text
case
default
if
else
for
while
int
float
char
double
```

because their specific rules occur before the generic identifier rule.

