# 32. What is `yylex()`?

`yylex()` is the **lexer function automatically generated by Lex/Flex**.

When you call:

```c
yylex();
```

the lexer starts reading input.

Conceptually:

```text
Input
  ↓
 yylex()
  ↓
Find matching pattern
  ↓
Execute action
  ↓
Find next pattern
  ↓
Execute action
  ↓
...
  ↓
End of input
```

So your `main()` doesn't manually scan the input.

**Flex generates `yylex()` for you.**

---

# 33. `yywrap()`

You have:

```c
int yywrap()
{
    return 1;
}
```

When Flex reaches the end of input, it calls `yywrap()`.

Returning:

```c
1
```

means:

> There is no more input. Stop scanning.

So:

```text
yywrap() → 1 → lexer terminates
```

---

# 34. Complete execution flow

Suppose you give this input:

```c
switch(x) {
    case 1:
        printf("One");
        break;

    case 2:
        printf("Two");
        break;

    default:
        printf("Invalid");
}
```

The lexer roughly works like this:

```text
switch
  ↓
matches "switch"
  ↓
print SWITCH
```

Then:

```text
(
  ↓
matches "("
  ↓
print Left parenthesis
```

Then:

```text
x
  ↓
matches identifier pattern
  ↓
print Identifier: x
```

Then:

```text
)
  ↓
Right parenthesis
```

Then:

```text
{
  ↓
Left brace
```

Then:

```text
case
  ↓
matches "case"
  ↓
case_count++
  ↓
case_count = 1
```

Then:

```text
1
  ↓
matches [0-9]+
  ↓
Integer constant
```

Then:

```text
:
  ↓
Colon
```

And so on.

When it reaches the second `case`:

```text
case_count = 2
```

When it reaches:

```text
default
```

it performs:

```text
default_count++
```

giving:

```text
default_count = 1
```

Finally, when input ends:

```text
yywrap()
  ↓
return 1
  ↓
yylex() finishes
  ↓
main() prints summary
```

